Chris joined Ayres Associates in 2006, bringing 10 years of experience
in civil engineering analysis, design, and construction. He has
comprehensive, substantial experience in corridor-type utilities, including
storm drainage and sanitary sewer and water systems. He provides
computer-based demand forecasting, master planning, capacity sizing,
preparation of detailed construction plans, review of plans on behalf of
local municipalities, and construction inspection services.

This is what Chris Matkins, water utilities manager for the City of
Loveland, Colorado, said about Chris’ work on wastewater flow
monitoring projects: “Ayres Associates works closely with our operations
crew; we have a high degree of trust in them. In fact, the trust is so high
that Chris is almost considered an extension of our staff; he is able to
make decisions and changes on the fly.”
Related Projects
Lake Minnehaha Remediation Plan, City of Cheyenne: Responsible
for project management. Project involved developing plan for ecological
remediation of historic Lake Minnehaha in Holliday Park. Lake has high
phosphorus and nitrogen levels that promote phytoplankton blooms, pH
levels higher than most aquatic species can tolerate, water temperatures
that exclude cold water fish, odors that indicate anoxic conditions in
sediment, and limited depth. Tasks included water quality sampling,
sediment sampling, locating site for disposal of dredged materials,
environmental analysis, and preparation of remediation plan.
Remediation of Historic Lake Minnehaha Holliday Park – Phase
1, City of Cheyenne: Responsible for project management. Project
involved taking concepts and action points from remediation plan to full
design. Items suggested in remediation plan were divided into Phase
1 tasks, which were primarily within boundary of Lake Minnehaha,
and Phase 2 tasks, which were outside of lake. Phase 1 implemented
deepening of lake, removal of island, upgrading lake’s outlet structure,
and outlet pipe and riprapping bank line.
.
Capitol Basin Storm Drain Assessment, City of Cheyenne:
Responsible for project management. Project involved confined space
entry into an 84-inch storm drain in City’s historic downtown area to try
to determine source of odors. Project team also collected water quality
samples from storm drains upstream.
Concrete Trail and Culvert Design, Town of Milliken, CO:
Responsible for overall direction of project, CDOT approval, and
coordination with client. Project involves designing concrete trail for Town
of Milliken, including a ditch crossing. Services include developing plans
and specifications to construct a concrete trail for project being funded
through grant from the Colorado Department of Transportation.
```

### Page 5 of 36

```text
Lake Minnehaha Remediation Plan Phase 2
City of Cheyenne
3
Crow Creek North Interceptor Sewer
Rehabilitation, Cheyenne Board of Public
Utilities: Responsible for project management.
Project involves design and construction services
for rehabilitation of 7,000 feet of 30-inch to 36-
inch concrete pipe sanitary sewer that has been
offline for several years. Project challenges include
groundwater infiltration, new flow diversion structure,
300 feet of new pipe, and installation of liner into
existing pipe. Design effort includes reviewing closed
circuit TV footage, confined space entry visual
inspection, survey, potholing, groundwater review
and modeling, new manholes, and coordination with
private property owners.
East Pershing Corridor Design and Construction,
City of Cheyenne: Project manager for sewer
and water design and installation. Project
involved providing roadway and utility design and
construction engineering for reconstruction of East
Pershing Boulevard from Dunn Avenue to Converse
Avenue, including reconstruction of East Pershing/
Converse Avenue/19th Street intersection as five-
leg, multi-lane roundabout. Project included public
outreach, right-of-way survey, plan for phased
construction to minimize traffic disruption, design of
modular-block and cast-in-place concrete retaining
walls in front of homes, and design of cantilevered
sign structures directing traffic to correct lanes at
roundabout. Also included were reconstruction of
portions of Pershing Boulevard, replacement of
water mains and associated valves and hydrants,
replacement of six sanitary sewer manholes and 490
linear feet of sanitary sewer, installation of two new
manholes, and relining or replacing additional 485
feet of sanitary sewer.
Lower Dry Creek Wetland, Laramie County
Planning & Development Office: Responsible for
water quality sampling. Project involved engineering
services for 2.5-acre forebay pond with 2.5-acre
wetland chase to provide sediment control and
preserve green space in area slated for urban
development. Prepared successful EPA-319 grant
application for funding for project, assisted with
detailed permitting efforts, coordinated efforts
between private property owner and Laramie
County, created design drawings for constructed
wetland, assisted with public information efforts,
prepared construction bid documents, conducted
water quality monitoring, and provided construction
management services.
Washington Avenue Phase 4 Final Design,
City of Loveland, CO: Responsible for project
management. Project involved alternative analysis
and final design for Phase 4 of Washington Avenue
storm drainage system. Services included hydrologic
and hydraulic analysis, storm drainage design,
water line design and replacement, preparation
of construction drawings and specifications, and
coordination with multiple City departments.
Significant utility crossings will include fiber optics,
sanitary sewer, and sensitive aging water lines.
Permits were obtained from railroads for jacking
and boring storm sewer and water lines under three
railroads. Water quality elements included four
large water quality vaults called Carlsonators, which
use extensive travel time to maximize settlement
of smaller suspended solids. Three additional
experimental water quality features were designed
by Ayres Associates and City: bio-box (small bio-
retention system); bio-basin (acts as filtration
system); and modified storm inlet, which allows flows
from smaller storm events to infiltrate into soil below
inlet. Project was constructed in downtown Loveland
in 2011. Construction of remaining portion of project
was slated to begin in May 2012. Construction
phase will have significant impact on large area of
downtown. Meetings provided home and business
owners with explanation of project.
```

