6
Florian Fiebig, EIT
Water Resources Specialist
Total Experience
2 Years
Registrations
Engineer Intern, FL
Education
MEng, Civil Engineering-Water
Resources and Hydraulics,
University of Florida, 2011
BS, Civil Engineering, University of
Florida, 2010
Florian joined Ayres Associates’ water resources engineering staff in
2012, bringing experience in hydraulics, hydrology, two- and three-
dimensional groundwater flow modeling, drainage and retention pond
design, reactive contaminant transport modeling, and stormwater runoff
monitoring. His experience includes monitoring wastewater effluent and
investigating river system hydrology, biochemical dynamics, and nutrient
levels.
Related Projects
Survey Monumentation, Photogrammetric Services, Major
Drainageway Planning for the Strasburg Area, Adams County, CO:
Staff engineer. Ayres Associates is assisting Adams County with drainage
and flood control improvements that will improve stormwater quality,
provide floodplain management and preservation, address current and
future erosion problems, and update hydrology for the watershed, among
other benefits. Services include creating new topographic mapping,
creating hydrologic and hydraulic models, evaluating Centennial Acres
tributary flood hazard, developing major drainageway plan for Centennial
Acres tributary, developing an outfall system plan for developed area
of Strasburg, revising the Special Flood Hazard Area to include the
Centennial Acres tributary, and facilitating formal public presentations.
Benson Park Pond and 29th Street Culvert Improvements, City
of Loveland, CO: Staff engineer. Project involves providing dredging
and construction plans for removal of sediment from City of Loveland’s
Benson Park pond and installation of new, properly sized outlet pipe on
29th Street side. Challenges included protecting sculptures in and near
pond, dense utility structures in 29th Street, and traffic volume on 29th
Street. In addition, no construction plans existed for pond, requiring
topographic survey.
Lewstone Creek-Scour Stabilization at Poudre Pipelines, City of
Fort Collins, CO: Staff engineer. Project involved designing erosion
control and protective cover for City of Fort Collins drinking water
pipelines where pipes cross Lewstone Creek. Damaging runoff had
destabilized creek channel and created headcut from confluence with
Poudre River upstream to the City’s pipelines, exposing one pipe.
Downstream from pipe crossing, Ayres designed partially grouted riprap
drop structure. Existing wall was used as groundwater cutoff wall and
was raised and extended laterally to address flanking issues. Due
to prevailing groundwater levels, toe of partially grouted riprap was
protected with launchable key of very large riprap that will arrest progress
of headcutting when it reaches protected reach of creek. Upstream
of cutoff wall, critical pipe crossing was stabilized and covered with
structural concrete cap to maintain existing channel bottom elevation,
which was only 6 inches above pipes.
```

### Page 9 of 36

```text
Lake Minnehaha Remediation Plan Phase 2
City of Cheyenne
7
Crow Creek North Interceptor Sewer
Rehabilitation, Cheyenne Board of Public
Utilities, WY: Staff engineer. Project involves design
and construction services for rehabilitation of 7,000
feet of 30-inch to 36-inch concrete pipe sanitary
sewer that has been offline for several years.
Project challenges include groundwater infiltration,
new flow diversion structure, 300 feet of new pipe,
and installation of liner into existing pipe. Design
effort includes reviewing closed circuit TV footage,
confined space entry visual inspection, survey,
potholing, groundwater review and modeling, new
manholes, and coordination with private property
owners.
Pleasant Valley Pipeline Forebay Sedimentation
Pond, City of Fort Collins, CO: Staff engineer.
Project involved adding sediment forebay to Pleasant
Valley Pipeline diversion from Pleasant Valley canal.
Work included civil/site design, structural design,
geotechnical investigation and design, hydraulic and
sediment analysis, project coordination, and design
documentation. Project followed massive summer
2012 forest fire that brought potential of generating
highly turbid runoff, possibly impacting City’s Poudre
River water supply. Ayres previously designed
screening system to keep sediment, leaves, pine
needles, and pine cones from clogging flow control
valves at treatment plants, which used to require
costly manual cleaning many times a day.
East Side Detention Facility, Pinnacle Consulting
Group, Inc., CO: Staff engineer. Project involves
analyzing Gray Lakes Reservoir area as possible
location for East Side Storage Facility as part of
Boxelder Basin Regional Stormwater Master Plan.
Amount of storage capacity will be analyzed to
determine feasibility of Gray Lakes and/or another
site upstream while accounting for irrigation storage
needs. One option is using existing dam material
to construct new dam farther west. Several options
will be considered related to hydrologic benefits
downstream. Phase 1 services include hydrographic
survey and investigation of property ownership
in and around proposed channel site between
CR 50 and CR 52. Hazard classification for Gray
Lakes Dam will be determined. Project will involve
developing rough grading plan, cross sections,
hydrologic model of proposed reservoir, updated
flow map, and cost estimate for Gray Lakes dam
embankment.
Hydraulic Modeling and Floodplain Management,
Sunstate Equipment Co.: Staff engineer. Project
involves providing hydraulic modeling and floodplain
management consulting services to guide expansion
of Sunstate Equipment building at 4228 Mulberry
Street in Fort Collins, Colorado, in accordance with
local floodplain regulations. Tasks include initial
consultation with Larimer County Flood Review
Board, data collection, hydraulic modeling analysis,
assessment of hydraulic impacts, and as-built
verification for building addition.
Emergency Water Line Protection, City of
Loveland, CO: Water resources specialist
responsible for water and wastewater assessments.
Two of three water lines feeding City of Loveland
had been damaged in September 2013 floods, and
remaining line was in danger of being undermined
and damaged by raging Big Thompson River, which
had changed course. Within four hours, Ayres team
and City staff had conceptual plan to push river back
to its original location. By following evening, river
had been moved back under very difficult flood-flow
conditions through earth-moving effort. Design and
permitting processes normally would have taken
years to complete. Ayres staff also joined Loveland’s
Emergency Operations Center (EOC), working all
hours to troubleshoot problems, particularly with
damaged utilities, and to begin navigating through
Federal Emergency Management Agency (FEMA)
process. Other staff assisted with geomorphic flood
damage assessment and determined peak flow rate
of event using high water measurements taken just
after flood.
Major Drainage Plan for Big Dry Creek
(Downstream of County Line Road), Urban
Drainage and Flood Control District, CO: Staff
engineer. Project involves fieldwork and development
and evaluation of all reasonable alternatives for
drainage and flood control plans for Big Dry Creek
downstream of County Line Road. Project will
include stormwater modeling; planning for drainage,
flood control, and stormwater quality; generation of
alternatives for improvements; and the drafting of a
final major drainageway conceptual design report.
```

