State University: Staff engineer. Project involved
developing strategy for flood mitigation for three
facilities on Iowa State University campus. Several
variables were considered in process of evaluating
flood mitigation alternatives. Recommended
solutions and provided cost estimates.
Subconsultant (Shive-Hattery) provided summary of
structural assessment of each building site.
Emergency Water Line Protection, City of
Loveland, CO: Water resources specialist
responsible for water and wastewater assessments.
Two of three water lines feeding City of Loveland
had been damaged in September 2013 floods, and
remaining line was in danger of being undermined
and damaged by raging Big Thompson River, which
had changed course. Within four hours, Ayres team
and City staff had conceptual plan to push river back
to its original location. By following evening, river
had been moved back under very difficult flood-flow
conditions through earth-moving effort. Design and
permitting processes normally would have taken
years to complete. Ayres staff also joined Loveland’s
Emergency Operations Center (EOC), working all
hours to troubleshoot problems, particularly with
damaged utilities, and to begin navigating through
Federal Emergency Management Agency (FEMA)
process. Other staff assisted with geomorphic flood
damage assessment and determined peak flow rate
of event using high water measurements taken just
after flood.
```

