### Page 23 of 36

```text
21
Lake Minnehaha Remediation Plan Phase 2
City of Cheyenne
S c o p e o f W o r k
After reading through the project’s scope of work,
Ayres Associates wholeheartedly agrees with
each aspect of the project and understands how
each item will enhance the water quality in Lake
Minnehaha and the beauty of Holliday Park while
simultaneously reducing flood damage within the
drainage basin.
Routing stormwater from the Duff Avenue line
through Lake Minnehaha will increase water
circulation in the lake. The addition of water quality
best management practices (BMPs) to the storm
sewer system upstream of the lake will keep trash,
oil, and sediment from polluting the lake and
becoming an eyesore.
Ayres has designed multiple storm sewer projects
within street corridors and will use our expertise
in preparing the preliminary design of the storm
sewer in Duff Avenue. The repairing of Duff Avenue
after laying the storm sewer will also be closely
considered. Due to the narrow nature of Duff
Avenue, it is anticipated that the entire street section
will be replaced (curb to curb) along most of the
alignment.
The preliminary design of the storm sewer from
Lake Minnehaha, up Duff Avenue to the box culvert
underneath Pershing Boulevard, is nearly a half-
mile long. Identifying and solving utility conflicts will
be the major challenge of the preliminary design.
Ayres anticipates that waterline lowerings, relocating
sanitary sewer lines, and moving sanitary sewer
drop manholes will solve most of the major conflicts.
Additionally, Ayres will consider multiple barrels and
elliptical pipe to avoid conflicts and to keep minimum
cover. When the major conflicts are recognized early
in the preliminary design phase, Ayres will order
utility potholing to determine exact depths of the
conflicting utilities to ensure a robust preliminary
design as a final product.
The topography from Pershing Boulevard generally
drops down to 19th Street; however, there is a high
point in the road at 22nd Street. The topographical
survey along with initial profile drawings of the
proposed storm sewer will reveal the shallow cover
north of 22nd Street and depth of pipe at the high
point at 22nd Street. Ayres is already thinking of
ideas to minimize the impact of deep trenching in
that vicinity of the preliminary design.
```

