Wa s h i n g t o n Av e n u e P h a s e 4 F i n a l D e s i g n
The City of Loveland retained Ayres Associates for a
five-year, open-ended contract. The first project under the
contract involved an alternative analysis and final design for
Phase 4 of the Washington Avenue storm drainage system.
Services for this project included hydrologic and hydraulic
analysis, storm drainage design, water line design and
replacement, preparation of construction drawings
and specifications, and coordination with multiple City
departments.
Significant utility crossings will include fiber optics, sanitary
sewer, and sensitive aging water lines. Permits were
obtained from railroads for jacking and boring storm sewer
and water lines under three railroads.
This project included several unusual water quality
elements. These included four large water quality vaults
called Carlsonators, which use an extensive travel time to maximize the settlement of smaller suspended solids.
Three additional water quality features were also designed: a bio-box, which is a small bio-retention system; a
bio-basin, which also acts as a filtration system; and a modified storm inlet, which allows flows from smaller storm
events to infiltrate into the soil below the inlet. These three water quality features are experimental and were
jointly designed by the City and Ayres Associates.
Ayres Associates staff previously completed the design of Phases 1, 2 and 3 of the Washington Avenue drainage
system project.
